+<body>
+<div data-shell-header></div>
+<main id="main">
+<div class="wrap"><ol class="crumbs"><li><a href="/">Portfolio</a></li><li><span aria-current="page">About</span></li></ol></div>
+  <header class="plate">
+    <div class="wrap plate__grid">
+      <div>
+        <p class="plate__kicker">About</p>
+        <h1>Candice Cantrelle</h1>
+        <p class="plate__lede">I work across simulation, machine learning, software, research and design, and I treat that range as one practice rather than several careers.</p>
+      </div>
+      <p class="stamp">Systems · Software · Simulation<br>Research · Games · Design<br><b>27</b> projects registered<br>Portfolio rebuilt 2026</p>
+    </div>
+    <div class="wrap"><hr class="plate__rule" style="margin-top:32px"></div>
+  </header>
+
+
+  <section class="doc">
+    <div class="wrap bio">
+      <div>
+        <img class="bio__portrait" src="/assets/images/site/headshot.webp" alt="Portrait of Candice Cantrelle" width="380" height="507" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
+        <p class="bio__caption">Candice Cantrelle</p>
+      </div>
+      <div>
+        <h2 class="sec-head"><span class="idx">01</span> What I do <span class="trail"></span></h2>
+        <p>
+          I build systems in order to understand them. That has taken the shape of nine browser
+          instruments that rebuild documented scientific experiments; a computer-vision pipeline
+          that turns a photograph of a key into a printable 3D model, shipped across four
+          delivery surfaces; machine-learning models for expression recognition, digit
+          classification and predictive maintenance; a three-tier research framework for
+          archaeological site detection; and, for several years before any of that, visual
+          systems for jewellery houses and property listings.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          Those look like unrelated fields. They are the same activity performed on different
+          material. Each one is a system with a boundary, a set of levers you can pull, and a
+          set of quantities you can only get at through instruments that are worse than the
+          thing they measure. Learning to be precise about that third category — what you
+          <em>cannot</em> see directly, and what it therefore costs to claim anything about it —
+          is the part that transfers.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          It is also why this portfolio is arranged the way it is. Every project carries a
+          status, every status has a written definition, and unfinished work is labelled
+          unfinished on the card rather than in a footnote nobody reads.
+        </p>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+  </section>
+
+  <section class="doc">
+    <div class="wrap">
+      <h2 class="sec-head"><span class="idx">02</span> How I work <span class="trail"></span></h2>
+      <p class="sec-hint">Four habits that show up in everything on this site, whatever the medium.</p>
+      <div class="grid grid--2">
+        <div class="habit">
+          <h3>Make the constraint visible</h3>
+          <p>
+            The simulations charge bench hours for measurements, so you have to decide what not
+            to look for. That is not a difficulty setting — it is the actual condition every
+            experimenter works under, and a model that hides it teaches the wrong lesson.
+          </p>
+        </div>
+        <div class="habit">
+          <h3>Report the thing that weakens the claim</h3>
+          <p>
+            The emotion detector's class imbalance sits next to its accuracy. The archaeology
+            framework opens by saying it has trained nothing. Where notebook outputs were lost,
+            the page says the number is unverifiable instead of quoting one anyway.
+          </p>
+        </div>
+        <div class="habit">
+          <h3>Build the smallest thing that answers the question</h3>
+          <p>
+            The turbofan model is a ridge regression, deliberately, so that the preprocessing and
+            the sensor-drift analysis are the visible work. The deepest SVHN network was
+            abandoned because its training cost made the search impractical, not because it
+            scored badly.
+          </p>
+        </div>
+        <div class="habit">
+          <h3>Design the container, not just the contents</h3>
+          <p>
+            The Simulations atlas has an evidence badge with five defined levels and a dashed
+            <em>status pending</em> state, because a schema that makes overclaiming awkward is
+            more reliable than an intention to be careful.
+          </p>
+        </div>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+  </section>
+
+  <section class="doc" id="credentials">
+    <div class="wrap">
+      <h2 class="sec-head"><span class="idx">03</span> Credentials <span class="trail"></span></h2>
+      <p class="sec-hint">
+        Thirteen certificate programmes across data science, AI engineering, full-stack
+        development, security, UX, project management, engineering mathematics and game
+        development. These are completed coursework, not degrees, and they are listed that way.
+      </p>
+      <p><a class="btn" href="/about/credentials/">Full credential list &amp; coursework</a></p>
+    </div>
+  </section>
+
+  <section class="doc">
+    <div class="wrap">
+      <h2 class="sec-head"><span class="idx">04</span> Contact <span class="trail"></span></h2>
+      <div class="contact-grid">
+        <div class="contact-card"><h3>Email</h3>
+          <a href="mailto:hello@candicecantrelle.com">hello@candicecantrelle.com</a></div>
+        <div class="contact-card"><h3>GitHub</h3>
+          <a href="https://github.com/Unicorn0-0Cakes" rel="noopener">Unicorn0-0Cakes</a></div>
+        <div class="contact-card"><h3>LinkedIn</h3>
+          <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/candice-cantrelle" rel="noopener">candice-cantrelle</a></div>
+        <div class="contact-card"><h3>Simulations atlas</h3>
+          <a href="https://unicorn0-0cakes.github.io/simulations/" rel="noopener">The instrument archive</a></div>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+  </section>
+
+  <section class="doc">
+    <div class="wrap">
+      <h2 class="sec-head"><span class="idx">05</span> Colophon <span class="trail"></span></h2>
+      <dl class="colophon" style="max-width:70ch">
+        <dt>Build</dt>
+        <dd>Static HTML, CSS and vanilla JavaScript served by GitHub Pages. No framework, no
+            bundler, no build step. Five stylesheets and two scripts, all of them readable.</dd>
+        <dt>Design system</dt>
+        <dd>Orbital — originally built for the Simulations atlas, extended here. Two authored
+            themes: <em>Field Manual</em> (day) and <em>Phosphor</em> (night). Each category
+            re-points three variables rather than introducing new colour.</dd>
+        <dt>Type</dt>
+        <dd>Space Grotesk and IBM Plex Mono, with a system serif for the Research wing.</dd>
+        <dt>Data</dt>
+        <dd>Every project on this site is listed in a curated registry at
+            <code>/data/projects.js</code>. Nothing is read from the GitHub API at runtime, so
+            the site does not change shape when a repository does.</dd>
+        <dt>Archive</dt>
+        <dd>The previous version of this site is preserved at <a href="/archive/">/archive/</a>,
+            unlinked and marked <code>noindex</code>. Nothing was deleted.</dd>
