+        <p class="q">Which architecture balances recognition accuracy against what it costs to train?</p>
+
+        <h2 class="case-h"><span class="n">01</span>What it is</h2>
+        <p>
+          A convolutional classifier that sorts facial photographs into four expressions —
+          happy, sad, neutral and surprise — using VGG16 as a frozen feature extractor with a
+          fine-tuned head. The work sits in affective computing: the attempt to get machines to
+          register something about human emotional state.
+        </p>
+
+        <h2 class="case-h"><span class="n">02</span>Why</h2>
+        <p>
+          Expressions are a good problem for learning what a classifier can and cannot do,
+          because humans disagree with each other about them. Happiness is easy — a smile is a
+          large, unambiguous change in facial geometry. Neutral is hard, because &ldquo;neutral&rdquo;
+          is defined by the absence of the features the model has learned to look for. That
+          asymmetry is visible in the results, and it is the interesting part.
+        </p>
+
+        <h2 class="case-h"><span class="n">03</span>My role</h2>
+        <p>
+          Dataset exploration and class-distribution analysis, architecture selection, the
+          training loop with per-run logging of hyperparameters and metrics, evaluation, and the
+          write-up. Rather than sweeping many architectures, this project committed to one and
+          iterated it, logging each run so the iterations could be compared against one another.
+        </p>
+
+        <h2 class="case-h"><span class="n">04</span>Results</h2>
+        <dl class="metrics">
+          <div class="metric"><dt>Test accuracy</dt><dd>72.66%</dd></div>
+          <div class="metric"><dt>Precision</dt><dd>75.27%</dd></div>
+          <div class="metric"><dt>Recall</dt><dd>72.66%</dd></div>
+          <div class="metric"><dt>F1 score</dt><dd>72.80%</dd></div>
+          <div class="metric"><dt>Validation accuracy</dt><dd>70.77%</dd></div>
+        </dl>
+        <p class="metric-note">
+          Figures as reported in the project notebook, 3 March 2025. These are the numbers the
+          model produced; they have not been rounded up or re-run to a better seed.
+        </p>
+        <p style="margin-top:var(--s-5)">
+          Validation and test accuracy sit within two points of each other, which suggests the
+          model generalises about as well as it fits — no severe overfitting, and no evidence
+          that a longer run would have closed the gap. Precision exceeding recall by roughly
+          three points means the model is somewhat conservative: when it commits to a class it is
+          more often right than its overall hit rate suggests, and it misses more than it
+          misfires.
+        </p>
+
+        <h2 class="case-h"><span class="n">05</span>What the numbers do not say</h2>
+        <p>
+          The dataset is not balanced. Happy, sad and neutral each account for roughly 26% of
+          the images; surprise is around 21%. A four-class problem with that distribution has a
+          majority-class baseline near 26%, so 72.66% is a real result rather than an artefact —
+          but the underrepresented class is also the one most likely to be carrying the error,
+          and a single overall accuracy figure hides that entirely.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          This is why the class distribution is reported here next to the accuracy rather than in
+          a methods appendix. An accuracy number without its class balance is not a claim anyone
+          can check.
+        </p>
