+      <div>
+        <p class="q">Where does added depth stop paying for itself?</p>
+
+        <h2 class="case-h"><span class="n">01</span>What it is</h2>
+        <p>
+          Digit classification on Street View House Numbers — photographs of house numbers
+          cropped from Google Street View, which is a harder problem than it sounds because the
+          crops contain neighbouring digits, motion blur, odd lighting and every typeface a sign
+          painter ever chose. Four architectures were built and compared on the same data: two
+          fully-connected networks and two convolutional ones.
+        </p>
+
+        <h2 class="case-h"><span class="n">02</span>Why</h2>
+        <p>
+          The stated goal was accuracy. The actual finding was about cost. It is easy to write a
+          comparison table in which the deepest model wins, and much more useful to record which
+          models you could actually train, tune and iterate on with the hardware you had.
+        </p>
+
+        <h2 class="case-h"><span class="n">03</span>My role</h2>
+        <p>
+          Dataset preparation and the train / validation / test split, all four architectures,
+          the training runs, the comparison, and the write-up.
+        </p>
+
+        <h2 class="case-h"><span class="n">04</span>The four models</h2>
+        <dl class="layers">
+          <div class="layer"><dt>ANN 1</dt><dd>
+            A baseline dense network. Adam at 0.001, categorical cross-entropy. Adequate on simple
+            classification, clearly out of its depth on images — no spatial structure to exploit,
+            so every pixel is an independent feature.</dd></div>
+          <div class="layer"><dt>ANN 2</dt><dd>
+            Deeper, with dropout and batch normalisation added. Training stabilised and
+            overfitting reduced, but the added parameters bought longer training rather than
+            better generalisation. Neither dense model was the right tool.</dd></div>
+          <div class="layer"><dt>CNN 3</dt><dd>
+            Convolutional layers with max pooling and Leaky ReLU. Immediate improvement — spatial
+            features are exactly what this problem has. Validation accuracy tracked training
+            accuracy closely, indicating minimal overfitting. <strong>The model I would
+            keep.</strong></dd></div>
+          <div class="layer"><dt>CNN 4</dt><dd>
+            Deeper and wider. Training required repeated restarts because of memory limits, which
+            made hyperparameter search impractical rather than merely slow. Abandoned — not for
+            scoring badly, but for being untunable on the hardware available.</dd></div>
+        </dl>
+
+        <h2 class="case-h"><span class="n">05</span>Outcome</h2>
+        <div class="no-metrics">
+          <strong>On the numbers</strong>
+          <p style="margin:0">
+            The notebook's own analysis reports the best convolutional model at roughly
+            <strong>97% accuracy</strong>. That figure is quoted from the write-up, not
+            re-verified: the notebook was committed with its cell outputs cleared, so no stored
+            evaluation output survives to check it against. It is recorded here as an
+            author-reported figure and should be read as one.
+          </p>
+        </div>
+        <p style="margin-top:var(--s-5)">
+          The comparison itself does not depend on that number. The ordering was unambiguous —
+          convolutional beat dense by a wide margin, and the deeper CNN was rejected on cost
+          rather than on score. CNN 3 gave the best ratio of accuracy to training time, and the
+          improvements it still wanted were cheap ones: batch normalisation for stability,
+          dropout against overfitting, augmentation for robustness.
+        </p>
+
+        <h2 class="case-h"><span class="n">06</span>Limitations</h2>
+        <ul>
+          <li>No stored metrics. Outputs were cleared before commit, so accuracy, loss curves and
+              per-class performance would all have to be regenerated to be citable.</li>
+          <li>The four models were not matched on parameter count or training budget, so the
+              comparison is between four designs rather than a controlled ablation.</li>
+          <li>CNN 4's ceiling is unknown. It was abandoned before it was tuned, so nothing can be
+              said about whether the extra depth would eventually have paid off with more
+              compute.</li>
+          <li>No comparison against a published SVHN baseline, so &ldquo;97%&rdquo; has no external
+              reference point attached to it here.</li>
+        </ul>
+
+        <h2 class="case-h"><span class="n">07</span>What I would do next</h2>
+        <p>
+          Re-run CNN 3 with outputs preserved, add a confusion matrix, and compare against a
+          published SVHN benchmark so the figure has something to sit beside. Then test whether
+          augmentation moves it at all — on a dataset this noisy, it plausibly will not, and a
+          null result there would be worth having.
+        </p>
+      </div>
+
+      <aside class="case__aside">
+<dl class="facts"><dt>Status</dt><dd>Showcase</dd><dt>Date</dt><dd>February 2025</dd><dt>Task</dt><dd>10-class digit classification</dd><dt>Dataset</dt><dd>Street View House Numbers</dd><dt>Architectures</dt><dd>2 dense, 2 convolutional</dd><dt>Framework</dt><dd>TensorFlow / Keras</dd><dt>Techniques</dt><dd>Dropout, batch norm, Leaky ReLU</dd><dt>Optimiser</dt><dd>Adam, lr 0.001 / 0.0005</dd><dt>Reported result</dt><dd>~97% (author-reported, unverified)</dd></dl><div class="facts"><dt style="font-family:var(--rf-mono);font-size:var(--t-nano);letter-spacing:var(--rf-track-s);text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--rf-muted);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:12px">Links</dt><ul class="linklist"><li><a href="https://github.com/Unicorn0-0Cakes/machine-learning/blob/main/CCantrelle2025_High_Code_SVHN_Digit_Recognition.ipynb" rel="noopener">Notebook &rarr;</a></li><li><a href="https://github.com/Unicorn0-0Cakes/machine-learning" rel="noopener">Repository &rarr;</a></li></ul></div>
+      </aside>
