+<body>
+<div data-shell-header></div>
+<main id="main">
+<div class="wrap"><ol class="crumbs"><li><a href="/">Portfolio</a></li><li><a href="/research/">Research</a></li><li><span aria-current="page">Multimodal Archaeology</span></li></ol></div>
+  <header class="plate">
+    <div class="wrap plate__grid">
+      <div>
+        <p class="plate__kicker">Case study · Research</p>
+        <h1>Multimodal Archaeological Site Detection</h1>
+        <p class="plate__lede">A three-tier detection pipeline, designed and scaffolded, with no dataset behind it and no result to report — which is the honest state of it and the reason it is here.</p>
+      </div>
+      <p class="stamp"><b>Research</b><br>Python · PyTorch<br>Conceptual prototype<br>2026</p>
+    </div>
+    <div class="wrap"><hr class="plate__rule" style="margin-top:32px"></div>
+  </header>
+
+
+  <section class="doc">
+    <div class="wrap case prose">
+      <div>
+        <p class="q">Could a staged detector triage satellite, LiDAR and archival text faster than survey alone — and how would you know?</p>
+
+        <div class="caveat">
+          <h2 style="font:inherit">Read this first</h2>
+          <p style="margin-bottom:0">
+            This project has produced no detection, segmentation or classification results. No
+            dataset has been acquired or processed end to end, no model has been trained on real
+            data, and the training script currently runs on placeholder inputs with dummy
+            declining loss values. Everything below describes an architecture and a set of design
+            decisions. None of it describes a validated system.
+          </p>
+        </div>
+
+        <h2 class="case-h"><span class="n">01</span>What it is</h2>
+        <p>
+          A proposed method for finding archaeological sites that nobody has recorded, by
+          combining data sources that are each individually insufficient: multispectral satellite
+          imagery, LiDAR-derived terrain products, and historical text. The proposal is a
+          three-tier cascade, where each tier is cheap relative to the one after it and exists to
+          reduce how much work the next tier has to do.
+        </p>
+
+        <h2 class="case-h"><span class="n">02</span>Why</h2>
+        <p>
+          Archaeological prospection is bounded by survey time. The area that might contain
+          something is enormous; the area anyone can walk is not. Any method that reliably
+          narrows the search is valuable even if its precision is poor, provided its recall is
+          good and its cost is low — which is a different optimisation target from the one most
+          detection models are built for, and the reason a cascade is the interesting shape.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          It is also a domain where a false positive and a false negative are not symmetrical
+          errors, and where the consequences of publishing coordinates are not purely academic.
+        </p>
+
+        <h2 class="case-h"><span class="n">03</span>My role</h2>
+        <p>
+          The architecture and its staging, the configuration system, the model cards, the
+          preprocessing scaffolding for three different data modalities, the pipeline
+          orchestration, and the documentation — including the decision to state the project's
+          incompleteness in the first line of its README rather than at the bottom.
+        </p>
+
+        <h2 class="case-h"><span class="n">04</span>The proposed pipeline</h2>
+        <dl class="layers">
+          <div class="layer"><dt>Tier 1 — Scan</dt><dd>
+            A modified YOLOv8 detector sweeping wide areas of multispectral satellite imagery to
+            flag points of interest quickly. Optimised for recall and throughput; precision is
+            explicitly the next tier's problem.</dd></div>
+          <div class="layer"><dt>Tier 2 — Delineate</dt><dd>
+            SegFormer over high-resolution patches centred on the flagged points, to produce
+            boundaries rather than points. Expensive per unit area, which is why it only ever
+            sees what Tier 1 hands it.</dd></div>
+          <div class="layer"><dt>Tier 3 — Characterise</dt><dd>
+            A Swin Transformer over fused modalities — imagery, LiDAR derivatives, processed text —
+            for classification and significance assessment. The tier where the multimodal claim
+            actually lives, and the least specified of the three.</dd></div>
+        </dl>
+
+        <h2 class="case-h"><span class="n">05</span>Implemented and not implemented</h2>
+        <div class="ledger">
+          <div class="ledger__col">
+            <h3>Implemented</h3>
+            <ul>
+              <li>Three-stage pipeline structure and an orchestration script that sets out the intended call sequence</li>
+              <li>YAML configuration management, global and per-model</li>
+              <li>Draft architecture definitions for all three models</li>
+              <li>Preprocessing scaffolding for Sentinel imagery, LiDAR and text</li>
+              <li>Model cards in <code>docs/</code> for each of the three components</li>
+              <li>MLflow experiment-tracking scaffolding</li>
+            </ul>
+          </div>
+          <div class="ledger__col ledger__col--open">
+            <h3>Not implemented</h3>
+            <ul>
+              <li>No dataset acquired, licensed or processed end to end</li>
+              <li><code>train.py</code> uses placeholder data loading, a placeholder optimiser and dummy declining loss values</li>
+              <li>No checkpoint has been produced from real training</li>
+              <li>Evaluation is not connected to held-out data or ground truth</li>
+              <li>No detection, segmentation or classification metrics exist</li>
+              <li>Several features described in the model cards are explicitly hypothetical</li>
+            </ul>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <h2 class="case-h"><span class="n">06</span>Outcome</h2>
+        <p>
+          What this project produced is a design and an honest account of its own state. That is
+          a smaller claim than the repository could have made — the scaffolding is complete
+          enough that it would have been easy to write about it in the present tense and let a
+          reader assume the rest.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          The more useful output was recognising why it stalled. The architecture was specified
+          top-down, all three tiers at once, before a single tier had touched real data. Three
+          model cards were written for models that had never been trained. The scope ran ahead of
+          the evidence, and the correct response to that is not to build the fourth thing.
+        </p>
+
+        <h2 class="case-h"><span class="n">07</span>Limitations</h2>
+        <ul>
+          <li>The three-tier cascade is a design proposal. It has not been validated as more
+              effective or more efficient than a simpler baseline, and no such comparison has
+              been run.</li>
+          <li>Data acquisition depends on external satellite, LiDAR and archival sources that are
+              not integrated. GDAL and Rasterio are notoriously difficult to install and are not
+              fully tested in this repository.</li>
+          <li>No ethical, legal or cultural-heritage review has been conducted. That review would
+              be required before this pipeline ran against real archaeological data, not
+              afterwards.</li>
+          <li>Archaeological site locations are sensitive information. Any future work on real
+              data has to handle the risk of disclosing exact coordinates, which is a design
+              constraint on the output format and not just an operational note.</li>
+        </ul>
+
+        <h2 class="case-h"><span class="n">08</span>The next reproducible milestone</h2>
+        <p>
+          Narrow, rather than extend. One small, lawful, publicly available dataset — a satellite
+          tile set with known reference sites. One real training loop, for Tier 1 only. One honest
+          baseline to compare against, such as a classical thresholding detector. One evaluation
+          with real precision and recall, reported plainly, including a null result if that is
+          what comes out. Then write down what worked and what did not, before adding a second
+          tier.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          If that milestone produces a Tier 1 detector that cannot beat simple thresholding, the
+          project has still answered its question. That outcome would be worth publishing here
+          exactly as readily as the other one.
+        </p>
+      </div>
+
+      <aside class="case__aside">
+<dl class="facts"><dt>Status</dt><dd>Research — conceptual prototype</dd><dt>Year</dt><dd>2026</dd><dt>Role</dt><dd>Architecture &amp; documentation</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Python</dd><dt>Models</dt><dd>YOLOv8, SegFormer, Swin Transformer</dd><dt>Framework</dt><dd>PyTorch</dd><dt>Tracking</dt><dd>MLflow (scaffolded)</dd><dt>Geospatial</dt><dd>GDAL, Rasterio</dd><dt>Config</dt><dd>YAML, per-model</dd><dt>Results</dt><dd>None. Nothing has been trained.</dd></dl><div class="facts"><dt style="font-family:var(--rf-mono);font-size:var(--t-nano);letter-spacing:var(--rf-track-s);text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--rf-muted);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:12px">Links</dt><ul class="linklist"><li><a href="https://github.com/Unicorn0-0Cakes/multimodal-archaeology-research-framework" rel="noopener">Repository &rarr;</a></li><li><a href="https://github.com/Unicorn0-0Cakes/multimodal-archaeology-research-framework/tree/main/docs" rel="noopener">Model cards &rarr;</a></li><li><a href="https://github.com/Unicorn0-0Cakes/multimodal-archaeology-research-framework#readme" rel="noopener">README &rarr;</a></li></ul></div>
+      </aside>
+    </div>
+  </section>
+</main>
