+
+  <section class="doc">
+    <div class="wrap">
+      <h2 class="sec-head"><span class="idx">01</span> What this is <span class="trail"></span></h2>
+      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1.1fr) minmax(0,1fr);gap:var(--s-8)">
+        <div>
+          <p>
+            These are not demonstrations. A demonstration shows you a result; an instrument
+            makes you go and get one. Every simulation here gives you a system, a set of
+            controls, and instruments that are worse than the system — noisy, partial,
+            expensive to use — because that is the situation the original experimenters
+            were actually in.
+          </p>
+          <p>
+            The consequence is that you can be wrong in them. You can spend your whole
+            measurement budget on the thing that turns out not to matter. You can lock in an
+            analysis and then find out what the accepted value was. That is the point.
+          </p>
+        </div>
+        <div>
+          <dl class="taxo">
+            <div><dt>Domain</dt><dd>Physics · Biology · Ecology · Society · Earth · Cognition</dd></div>
+            <div><dt>Mode</dt><dd>What you actually do: Observe · Diagnose · Design · Measure · Infer</dd></div>
+            <div><dt>Model</dt><dd>Agent-based · Differential equation · Statistical · Historical reconstruction</dd></div>
+            <div><dt>State</dt><dd>Playable · Research preview · Development</dd></div>
+          </dl>
+        </div>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+  </section>
+
+  <section class="doc">
+    <div class="wrap">
+      <h2 class="sec-head"><span class="idx">02</span> How to read an evidence badge <span class="trail"></span></h2>
+      <p class="sec-hint">
+        Every instrument carries exactly one. It says what kind of thing the model is, so that
+        &ldquo;this simulation shows&hellip;&rdquo; can be checked rather than taken on trust. A new instrument
+        renders a dashed <em>status pending</em> chip until its methods page can show the work — visibly
+        unfinished beats quietly overclaiming.
+      </p>
+      <dl class="evidence-key">
+        <div><dt>Historical reconstruction</dt><dd>Rebuilds a documented experiment.</dd></div>
+        <div><dt>Established mathematical model</dt><dd>Published equations with known behaviour.</dd></div>
+        <div><dt>Calibrated research model</dt><dd>Tuned against published benchmarks; the table is shown.</dd></div>
+        <div><dt>Exploratory agent-based model</dt><dd>Dramatises a mechanism. The numbers illustrate; they do not measure.</dd></div>
+        <div><dt>Uncalibrated prototype</dt><dd>Internally consistent. Nothing has been fitted to data.</dd></div>
+      </dl>
+    </div>
+  </section>
+
+  <section class="doc">
+    <div class="wrap">
+      <h2 class="sec-head"><span class="idx">03</span> The catalogue <span class="trail"></span></h2>
+      <p class="sec-hint">
+        Each card opens the instrument itself, on the atlas site where it lives. Every instrument
+        also has a methods page documenting its model, its assumptions and what it does not do.
+      </p>
+      <div class="grid" id="sims"></div>
+    </div>
+  </section>
+
+  <section class="doc">
+    <div class="wrap">
+      <div class="atlas-cta">
